    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698985/research-fellow-in-codesign-behaviour-change-qualitative-research": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Co-Design, Behaviour Change & Qualitative Research",
      "description": "Research Fellow in Co-Design, Behaviour Change & Qualitative Research Job No.: 698985 Location: St Kilda Road, Melbourne Employment Type: Part-time, fraction (0.8) Duration: 12-month fixed-term appointment Remuneration: Pro-rata of $86,195 - $116,981 pa Level A (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact as a Research Fellow shaping safer, healthier and more sustainable cities Join an inclusive, collaborative community turning research into real-world change Be inspired by extraordinary ideas transforming active travel and urban road safety The Opportunity Join a multidisciplinary research team as a Research Fellow and help shape healthier, safer and more sustainable ways of moving around our cities. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to applied research exploring road safety, active travel and behaviour change, working alongside communities, local councils, government and health services to develop practical solutions that improve urban environments. The position will contribute to the co-design and evaluation of active travel interventions, bringing together community perspectives and research evidence to inform policy and practice. Key activities include facilitating stakeholder workshops, conducting interviews and surveys, analysing qualitative and quantitative data and translating findings into research publications, evaluation reports and practical resources. The role will also build collaborative research partnerships and coordinate project activities across multiple initiatives. We are seeking someone with an honours degree or higher qualification in a relevant discipline and/or be progressing towards a doctorate, or hold a doctoral qualification in a relevant field such as public health, behavioural or social science, urban planning or transport planning. Experience in qualitative research, stakeholder engagement, co-design workshops and survey methods will be highly relevant, alongside strong analytical, organisational and communication skills. An interest in active transport, road safety and healthier urban environments will be valuable. If you are passionate about research that connects communities, evidence and real-world change, we encourage you to apply. Monash University seeks applicants for this opportunity who are able to demonstrate present valid and current Australian work rights. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Dr Lauren Pearson, Research Fellow & Equity Lead, Healthy Active Cities, Lauren.Pearson@monash.edu Position Description: Research Fellow Applications Close: Sunday 1 November 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/699242/senior-indigenous-academic-engagement-officer": {
      "title": "Senior Indigenous Academic Engagement Officer",
      "description": "Senior Indigenous Academic Engagement Officer Job No.: 699242 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 2 year fixed-term appointment Remuneration: $110,527 - $121,227p.a. HEW Level 6 plus 17% employer superannuation Identified role. Open to Indigenous applicants only Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community The Opportunity Are you passionate about empowering First Nations students and creating meaningful change? Join the William Cooper Institute to lead programs like the Indigenous Academic Enhancement Program (IAEP) and the Monash Indigenous Access Program (MIAP), supporting students on their journey to success. As the Senior Indigenous Academic Engagement Officer, you’ll coordinate initiatives, monitor student progress, and provide personalised support that makes a real difference. With your expertise, you’ll help shape strategies that celebrate and advance Indigenous knowledge, culture, and achievement. We’re seeking a dedicated professional with relevant qualifications or extensive experience to drive these impactful programs. Be part of a dynamic team committed to breaking barriers and fostering First Nations excellence. Your leadership could inspire the next generation—apply today and help us make a lasting impact! About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Erin Pennett, Director, William Cooper Institute, +61 0419 288 968 Position Description: Senior Indigenous Academic Engagement Officer Applications Close: Sunday 1st November 11:55pm AEDT Please submit your application as soon as possible for this fabulous opportunity to join Monash University. Interviews will be held as strong applicants are identified. Applications will close when the role has been filled. Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/699402/phd-research-opportunity-public-policy-for-carbon-upcycling-and-australias-circular-economy": {
      "title": "PhD Research Opportunity – Public Policy for Carbon Upcycling and Australia’s Circular Economy",
      "description": "PhD Research Opportunity – Public Policy for Carbon Upcycling and Australia’s Circular Economy Job No.: 699402 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 3.5 years fixed-term appointment Remuneration: The successful applicant will receive a Research Living Allowance, at current value of $41,100AUD per annum 2027 full-time rate (tax-free stipend), indexed plus allowances as per RTP stipend scholarship conditions at: www.monash.edu/graduate-research/study/scholarships/fees-scholarships/scholarship-policy-and-procedures . A tuition fee scholarship and Single Overseas Health Cover (OSHC) will be provided for a successful international awardee. The Opportunity Australia generates significant quantities of underutilised carbon waste, including agricultural residues, waste plastics and end-of-life tyres. These materials are often stockpiled, sent to landfill or exported, representing both an environmental challenge and an opportunity to develop new value-added products and more sustainable manufacturing pathways. The ARC Research Hub for Value-Added Processing of Underutilised Carbon Waste (VAPUCW) is developing innovative technologies to transform these underutilised carbon resources into valuable products. The Hub brings together researchers across multiple disciplines and leading universities with industry partners to advance waste upcycling, improve technology readiness and support the transition towards a circular economy. This PhD opportunity will contribute to the Hub's research program, providing the successful candidate with the opportunity to undertake research in an interdisciplinary and industry-connected environment. This PhD project contributes to the Hub’s research program and investigates policy frameworks required to accelerate Australia’s transition toward a circular economy, focusing on carbon upcycling and emerging chemical recycling technologies. Through comparative case studies, the project examines the systemic governance challenges and multi-sector policy mechanisms needed to operationalize circularity across domestic supply chains. The successful candidate will be based in the School of Social Sciences under the supervision of Professor Jo Lindsay, AProf Martin Geissdoerfer and Prof Michael Mintrom and will have the opportunity to collaborate with researchers and industry partners across the ARC Research Hub. Monash University is a leading global research and education institution known for turning ideas into real-world impact. It is a public research university and a member of Australia’s Group of Eight. Globally, it is ranked 31st. As the largest Australian university, Monash also has international campuses in Italy, China, India, Indonesia, and Malaysia. Monash conducts world-class research in partnership with industry and leading institutions. Graduate researchers work on real-world challenges in a supportive environment with access to advanced facilities. Candidate Requirements We are seeking a candidate with a desirable experience in: A strong interest in sustainability transition governance and public policy Strong communication and problem-solving skills The ability to work both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary research team In its assessment, the selection committee will prioritise applicants who hold an Australian (or equivalent international) Honour’s or Master’s degree (both in a relevant field), with a significant research component and with first-class honours/H1 awarded. Details of eligibility requirements, including English-language proficiency skills, to undertake a PhD in the Faculty of Arts are available at www.monash.edu/arts/graduate_research/how-to-apply . Applicants should ensure they familiarise themselves with these requirements before deciding whether they should apply. Scholarship holders must be enrolled full-time and on campus. Please note: applicants who already hold a PhD will not be considered. The successful applicant will be expected to enrol by 1 February 2027 . However, there may be some flexibility as to the date of commencement. Enquiries You are required to discuss your research proposal with Professor Jo Lindsay, Email: jo.lindsay@monash.edu before you submit an application. How to Apply Applications shall comprise: A cover letter that includes a brief statement of the applicant’s suitability A draft Research Proposal that fits within the broad project aims and objectives, and demonstrates some understanding of the area of research A detailed curriculum vitae, including a list of any published works, conference presentations and relevant work experience A full statement of academic records, degree certificates and official grading system (if available), supported by scanned copies of relevant certified documentation English language proficiency test results (if applicable) Names and contact details of 2 academic referees Once you have discussed your proposal with Professor Jo Lindsay, please submit your application via My.App and upload all supporting documentation in pdf format: Select Admission Only as the Application Type Select ‘ Yes ’ to indicate that you are applying for a scholarship for this research and enter “PhD Research Opportunity – Public Policy for Carbon Upcycling and Australia’s Circular Economy” as the scholarship name under Research Program Funding of the application. Select Professor Jo Lindsay as the proposed main supervisor (final supervision arrangements to be confirmed upon appointment). Application Close: Sunday 15 November 2026, 11:55 pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/698926/postdoctoral-research-fellow-future-lives": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Future Lives",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Future Lives Job No.: 698926 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 3 year fixed-term appointment Remuneration: $123,138 - $146,228 pa Level B (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity Are you an early-career researcher passionate about gender equity, Indigenous studies, and transformative community-led research? Join an interdisciplinary team at the ARC Centre of Excellence for the Elimination of Violence Against Women (CEVAW). As the Postdoctoral Research Fellow on the landmark Future Lives and Violence Against Women (VAW) project, you will lead essential fieldwork exploring the paid and unpaid workforce resources tackling violence against women across the Pacific Island countries and Papua New Guinea. This is a unique opportunity to co-design culturally grounded analytical frameworks, directly inform workforce training, and collaborate with key partner organisations in complex, nuanced, and culturally sensitive environments. About the Project The Australia Research Council Centre of Excellence for the Elimination of Violence against Women (CEVAW) is the world’s first Centre to tackle the full range of forms of violence against women in Australia and the Indo-Pacific region. CEVAW aims to transform our understanding of the problem by examining the structural drivers that cause and compound violence against women (VAW), and pioneering new, evidence-based approaches to inform trajectory-altering practice and policy. An opportunity exists to join CEVAW as a Postdoctoral Research Fellow within the Future Lives and Violence Against Women (VAW) workstream to contribute experience in Indigenous-centred research methodologies to our understanding of the nature of the paid and unpaid workforce in current and future Indigenous family violence sector across the Pacific and Papua New Guinea. This research will generate evidence and insights to inform the working conditions and skills of the future workforce responding to violence against women. Why This Role Matters In this role, you will bridge high-impact academic research and real-world outcomes by: Leading On-the-Ground Research: Direct fieldwork, data collection, and qualitative data management across multiple Pacific and PNG sites. Co-Designing Frameworks: Develop an analytical audit framework that respects local contexts and translates directly into training and support for the front-line Indigenous family violence workforce in the Pacific and PNG. Building Future Capability: Co-supervise research support staff and mentor HDR students while contributing to CEVAW’s research training and ethics programs. Publishing and Innovating: Formulate high-impact publications, present at international conferences, and contribute to competitive research funding proposals. About You We are seeking a collaborative, highly organised researcher with a strong diplomatic approach and deep respect for Indigenous-centred methodologies. Key Qualifications and Expertise: Education: A PhD in Indigenous Studies, Gender Studies, or a related discipline relevant to the project. Community Engagement: Demonstrated experience working collaboratively, diplomatically, and ethically with Indigenous communities across the Pacific and PNG on sensitive issues. Research and Methodologies: Strong knowledge of interdisciplinary and Indigenous-centred research approaches, supported by a growing track record of peer-reviewed publications. Teamwork and Leadership: Ability to work both independently to meet project deadlines and collegially within an interdisciplinary team. Communication: Exceptional written and oral communication skills, with experience using relevant analytical software. What We Offer Mentorship: Work directly alongside leading scholar Professor Kyllie Cripps in a supportive, high-performing research environment. Collaboration: Engage directly with CEVAW Chief Investigators, Partner Investigators and international partner organisations. Career Growth: Opportunities to build your publishing portfolio, apply for competitive funding, develop supervisory experience, and present at national and international forums About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Professor Kylie Kripps, School of Social Sciences, Kyllie.Cripps@monash.edu Position Description: Research Fellow - Future Lives Applications Close: Thursday 12th November 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Please submit your application as soon as possible for this fabulous opportunity to join Monash University. Interviews will be held as strong applicants are identified. Applications will close when the role has been filled. Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
