    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/699288/senior-technical-officer-school-of-translational-medicine": {
      "title": "Senior Technical Officer - School of Translational Medicine",
      "description": "Senior Technical Officer - School of Translational Medicine Job No.: 699288 Location: The Alfred Centre Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 12-month fixed-term contract Remuneration: $110,527 - $121,227 pa HEW Level 07 (plus 17% employer superannuation) Be inspired, every day Enjoy the freedom to discover something new Take your career in exciting, rewarding directions There’s a certain feeling you get from working at Monash University. It’s the feeling that you’re a part of something special. Something significant. So if you’re looking for the next chapter in your career, it’s here. You’ll be given the opportunity to challenge yourself, build on your skills, and make a significant contribution to a workplace that’s filled with energetic and inspiring people. Talent thrives here – and so do truly satisfying careers. The Faculty of Medicine, Nursing and Health Sciences (FMNHS) is the largest faculty at Monash University, a global university, offering the most comprehensive suite of professional health training in Victoria. Our vision is to improve the human condition through education, medical research, and aspiring to lead in these areas. By collaborating to influence local, national and international policy we aim to improve health and social outcomes. The Opportunity The Monash University School of Translational Medicine (STM) is the FMNS’s leading clinical school for biomedical research and education, offering undergraduate and postgraduate study programs. Translational and clinical research in STM covers a great breadth of subject areas, with 16 different departments and centres that encourage multidisciplinary research and attract elite students, researchers and teachers to its well-equipped research laboratories and facilities. The school partners with industry to put research into practice and contribute to the treatment and cure of several specific diseases and conditions. Further details can be found at: www.monash.edu/medicine/ccs . Reporting to the School Research & Operations Manager, the Senior Technical Officer (STO) is responsible for overseeing and delivering a range of significant and complicated research-related activities. You will be expected to provide high level laboratory and facility operations support for all researchers, staff, students and onsite facilities. As the successful candidate you will be responsible for: Maintenance and oversight of the physical resources of the school, research ethics monitoring, compliance and laboratories adherence to OHS&E policies Providing strategic and operational advice to clients and delivering efficient technical services Developing and maintaining up to date specialist or technical knowledge of new and innovative compliance frameworks and accreditation requirements Budget management, planning and contributing to funding proposals Managing the ongoing equipment service contracts To be successful in the role, you will have a degree qualification in a relevant field with extensive relevant experience as well as experience in overseeing a successful technical service, program or facility, with a focus on operational excellence and experience in supporting a safe operating environment, implementing OHS requirements. If this sounds like a position that suits your current career focus we look forward to hearing from you. This role is a full-time position; however, flexible working arrangements may be negotiated. Your application must address the selection criteria. Please refer to \" How to apply for Monash Jobs \". At Monash University, we are committed to being a Child Safe organisation. This position at the University will require the incumbent to hold a valid Working with Children Check and a satisfactory Police Check. We encourage applications from First Nations people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodiverse people, and people of all genders, sexualities and age groups. Join us in our commitment to ensuring a better future for ourselves and for the communities in which we study, work, live and engage – #ChangeIt with us. Enquiries: Dr Michelle Zajac, School Manager, School of Translational Medicine, +61 3 9903 0669 or michelle.zajac@monash.edu Position Description: Senior Technical Officer Applications Close: Tuesday 17 November 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/699386/manager-research-operations": {
      "title": "Manager, Research Operations",
      "description": "Manager, Research Operations Job No.: 699386 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: 6-months fixed-term Remuneration: $124,343 - $137,251 pa HEW Level 08 (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity An exciting opportunity has arisen within the Faculty of Information Technology for a strategic and experienced Research Operations Manager. In this pivotal leadership role, you will be at the forefront of driving operational excellence across the Faculty’s research activities. Reporting to the Group Manager (Research & Graduate Research), you will lead the operations function within the research portfolio, with responsibility for developing and implementing efficient, compliant, and scalable systems, processes, and resources that underpin all research support functions. You will collaborate closely with the Deputy Dean (Research), Associate Dean (Graduate Research), and other senior academic and professional staff to support strategic planning, governance, and the smooth execution of research and funding activities. Your focus will be on improving and streamlining research operations, with particular emphasis on administration, automation, and service efficiency. You will lead post-award processes and ensure robust compliance, risk management, and quality assurance frameworks are in place. You will also oversee strategic budget planning for the research portfolio, managing a budget of up to $5 million and ensuring accurate forecasting and resource allocation. You bring significant experience in research or academic operations within a large, complex organisation. With strong leadership, business process, and research management capabilities, you’re adept at managing systems, leading teams, and working collaboratively with diverse stakeholders. Your data-driven approach and strategic mindset will enable you to drive meaningful improvements across the research operations landscape. This is your opportunity to lead impactful change and shape the future of research support in a globally recognised, forward-thinking IT Faculty. Join a collaborative team that’s committed to innovation, service excellence, and research success. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something groundbreaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity , and welcome and celebrate everyone's contributions, lived experience and expertise. That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Learn more about Monash . Join the pursuit of our purpose to build a better future for ourselves and our communities - #ChangeIt with us. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Steve Gardner, Group Manager Research and Graduate Research, steven.gardner@monash.edu Position Description: Manager, Research Operations Applications Close: Tuesday 10 November 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/699039/senior-application-administrator": {
      "title": "Senior Application Administrator",
      "description": "Senior Application Administrator Job No.: 699039 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Fixed-term appointment until 1 September 2027 Remuneration: $124,343 - $137,251 pa HEW Level 08 (plus 17% employer superannuation), although a competitive remuneration package can be applied for an experienced candidate Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them Join our team as a Senior Application Administrator and play a key role in maintaining the performance, security and reliability of our SAP environment. Working alongside SAP functional teams, developers, infrastructure specialists and vendors, you will support day-to-day operations while contributing to upgrades, integrations and broader technology initiatives. About the role In this role, you will: Administer, monitor and support SAP development, test and production environments Monitor system performance, availability, capacity, interfaces and background jobs Investigate and resolve technical incidents and system issues Manage SAP transports and support change and release activities Coordinate system upgrades, patching, kernel updates and support packs Support system copies, client refreshes, backup and disaster-recovery activities Maintain certificates, secure connectivity and SAP integration points Work with internal teams and external vendors to resolve complex technical issues Identify opportunities to improve system performance, resilience and automation Contribute to SAP projects, migrations and implementation of new capabilities About you You will bring: Experience working in an SAP BASIS administration or SAP technical operations role Strong knowledge of SAP system administration, monitoring and troubleshooting Experience supporting SAP S/4HANA and/or SAP ECC environments Experience with SAP HANA, transport management, upgrades, patching and system refreshes An understanding of SAP security, certificates, integrations, databases and infrastructure Strong analytical and problem-solving skills The ability to communicate effectively with technical and non-technical stakeholders A collaborative approach and the ability to manage competing priorities Experience with SAP Fiori, SAP Gateway, SAP Cloud Connector, SAP Business Technology Platform, cloud-hosted SAP environments or SAP Cloud ALM would be highly regarded. Why join us? This is an opportunity to work across a complex and evolving SAP environment, contribute to strategic technology initiatives and expand your technical expertise within a supportive and collaborative team. We offer flexible working arrangements, professional development opportunities and the chance to make a meaningful contribution to the reliability and future direction of our SAP landscape. If you are an experienced SAP BASIS professional looking for your next opportunity, we would like to hear from you. Please refer to the attached Position Description for further information about the role and application requirements. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Mr Cedric Lim, Capability Lead, +61 3 9903 1040 Position Description: Senior Application Administrator Applications Close: Thursday 5 November 2026, 11:55pm AEST Please submit your application as soon as possible for this fabulous opportunity to join Monash University. Interviews will be held as strong applicants are identified. Applications will close when the role has been filled. Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/699507/community-engagement-officer": {
      "title": "Community Engagement Officer",
      "description": "Community Engagement Officer Job No.: 699507 Location: 509 St Kilda Road, Melbourne Employment Type: Part-time, fraction (0.5) Duration: 12-month fixed-term appointment (with potential for extension) Remuneration: Pro-rata of $86,344 - $99,179 pa HEW Level 05 (plus 17% employer superannuation) Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community Be surrounded by extraordinary ideas - and the people who discover them The Opportunity Commencing in January 2027 and reporting to the Chief Investigator Dr Lidia Engel, the Community Engagement Officer provides high-quality administrative and professional support for the RISE (Reducing Inequalities and Promoting Social Connection to Enable Healthy Ageing Among Older Adults in Rural Australia) project within the School of Public Health and Preventive Medicine. Key Responsibilities: Establish and maintain strong partnerships with community organisations, participants, and consumers with lived experience. Coordinate participant recruitment, retention, and Community Advisory Group/co-design activities. Plan and deliver community-focused communication and knowledge translation activities, including workshops, newsletters, and community forums. Manage project administrative processes, data input/analysis, and report generation in line with privacy and compliance standards. About You: A relevant tertiary qualification or equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education/training. Strong organizational, administrative, and problem-solving skills with a proven ability to manage project priorities. Demonstrated commitment to stakeholder and consumer engagement, with excellent written and verbal communication skills across diverse audiences. Highly developed computer literacy, including experience with business software such as Microsoft Office. Willingness to travel to rural communities across Australia as required. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity . That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must include your most recent CV plus a cover letter outlining your fit for the role and addressing the following Key Selection Criteria in the position description: KSC #2: Project coordination and organisational capability KSC #7: Communication and interpersonal skills KSC #9: Community engagement and stakeholder relationships Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disabilities, neurodivergent people, and people of all genders, sexualities, and age groups. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Lidia Engel, Chief Investigator / Senior Research Fellow, School of Public Health and Preventive Medicine, lidia.engel@monash.edu Position Description: Community Engagement Officer Applications Close: Wednesday 17 November 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/513/cw/en/job/697738/associate-professor-of-psychology-indigenous": {
      "title": "Associate Professor of Psychology - Indigenous",
      "description": "Associate Professor of Psychology - Indigenous Job No.: 697738 Location: Clayton campus Employment Type: Full-time Duration: Continuing appointment Remuneration: $181,629 - $200,092 Level D (plus 17% super) Identified role. Open to Indigenous applicants only Amplify your impact at a world top 50 University Join our inclusive, collaborative community The Opportunity Reporting to the Head of School, the focus of this role will be to lead both research and teaching for the advancement of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People’s social and emotional well-being. As the successful candidate you will be responsible for providing academic leadership of the Murrup Bung-allambee Indigenous Psychology Group, guiding its research agenda, mentoring Indigenous students and staff engaged with the group, and continuing its work advancing culturally responsive and decolonial approaches to psychology teaching and practice. The appointee will have a doctoral qualification and/or recognised significant experience in the relevant discipline area. About Monash University At Monash , work feels different. There’s a sense of belonging, from contributing to something ground breaking – a place where great things happen. We value difference and diversity , and welcome and celebrate everyone's contributions, lived experience and expertise. That’s why we champion an inclusive and respectful workplace culture where everyone is supported to succeed. Some 20,000 staff work for Monash around the world. We have 95,000 students, four Australian campuses, and campuses in Malaysia and Indonesia. We also have a major presence in India and China, and a significant centre and research foundation in Italy. In our short history, we have skyrocketed through global university rankings and established ourselves consistently among the world's best tertiary institutions. We rank in the world’s top-50 universities in rankings including the QS World University Rankings 2026. Together with our commitment to academic freedom , you will have access to quality research facilities, infrastructure, world-class teaching spaces, and international collaboration opportunities. Learn more about Monash . Today, we have the momentum to create the future we need for generations to come. Accelerate your change here. Monash supports flexible and hybrid working arrangements. We have a range of policies in place enabling staff to combine work and personal commitments. This includes supporting parents . To Apply For instructions on how to apply, please refer to ' How to apply for Monash Jobs '. Your application must address the Key Selection Criteria. Diversity is one of our greatest strengths at Monash. We are committed to fostering an inclusive and accessible recruitment process at Monash. If you need any reasonable adjustments, please contact us at hr-recruitment@monash.edu in an email titled 'Reasonable Adjustments Request' for a confidential discussion. Your employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of all pre-employment and/or background checks required for the role, as determined by the University. Enquiries: Professor Shantha Rajaratnam, Head of School, School of Psychological Sciences +61 3 (9905 3934) Position Description: Associate Professor of Psychology - Level D - Indigenous Applications Close: Tuesday 24 November 2026, 11:55pm AEDT Supporting a diverse workforce Back to search results Email Job Apply now Monash University recognises that its Australian campuses are located on the unceded lands of the people of the Kulin nations, and pays its respects to their elders, past and present."
